u Regulatory and compliance management and assumption of responsibilities and answerability in their respective countries with mutual co-operation; u Dispute resolution mechanisms including buy-out of one partner by the other at a mutually agreed price subject to a non-compete covenant. In India, as in UK and the USA, the non-compete covenant holds if it is for a short term of about two years to secure a swift Court injunction restraining the violating side setting out to compete during the period.

As already mentioned, the sector of JVs is essentially of the latest overseas-technology oriented brought into instant use in India without its transfer for licenced production to an Indian party, a concept of immense value in technological knowledge-sharing and in saving the time otherwise taken in indigenisation and the related sunk costs both lending to give a competitive edge. In order to afford greater role in the indigenous manufacture, a policy change was announced by the Government in 2002 allowing 26% FDI with a view to throwing open a part of the Rs. 40,000 crore annual defence procurement sourcing by imports to Indian manufacturing companies, and some ten of them including Mahindra & Mahindra, L&T and Tata Motors that claimed capability to make body armour, bullet-proof clothing, mobile launchers, avionics, simulators, radars and special purpose vehicles etc. were issued LOIs. It would, however, appear that there has been no progress in the last three years and no JVs have been formed as the methodology of implementation of the change in the policy is under examination by Mr. Vijay Kelkar who has been entrusted with it.

Joint Venture Arrangements/Agreements

29.2 As in a better organized partnership arrangement, invariably every Joint Venture Company is founded on a well thought out and carefully written agreement on the models provided by the chambers of commerce in their countries keenly sponsored by the Governments, essentially of the character of a refined partnership deed adapted to the incidents of company-type operation in India as an unregistered document, defining the commercial and control relativities between the partners in a legal casting compatible as far as possible with the systems of civil laws in force of the partnering countries, as the JV’s charter independent of the company but made between the persons who are desirous to associate in it and run with it, before it is formed. The JV agreement encompasses all aspects of the intended business in its essential detail crafted by professionals and vetted by merchant bankers taking into account repatriation benefits having regard to the tax treaties in force with India, covering:

u The kind of company to be formed, whether public or private and whether by acquisition or demerger of an existing Indian company and/or formation of a new company as the JV;

u IPRs: JVs are formed largely for the exploitation of foreign state-of-the-art technology ready to turn to account in the vast Indian markets and partake the character of the ‘letters patent’ companies of old times (see Ch. 3) for permissive user of strategic foreign and Indian partners forming the enterprise in various ways of financial and other joint participation. One area of immense financial risk and complexity in Indian manufacture that exists as inherent in it is the danger of the foreign partner switching off the power supply unannounced for a single product venture, and for a multi-product venture of diverse technologies it borders more or less to the problem in the segregation of the generation and application of nuclear energy between civil and military purposes in a ‘sealed black box’ mode. The problem has the potential to turn out to be knotted defying any practical solution in the area of Research and Development for advances in such permissive technologies attempted in the name of the JV in India involving huge outlays. Section 35 of the Income-tax Act allows the outlay both on revenue and capital account to be accumulated as a loss asset subject to some conditions with a provision to be carried forward at par with unabsorbed depreciation against future profits to be tax-free due to the deferred tax benefits of the time differentials. But this is limited to 8 years succeeding the assessment year at present (section 72 of the said Act) to the full extent of which the JV may or may not last and the outlay may be just left to remain as an absolute cash loss in the books. On the other hand, there is no legal framework to separate the results of the advances in the technology part achieved at the cost of the JV in India for patent protection otherwise than as contractual rights inter se the JV Partners. This could form a China Wall between them in running the JV smoothly, and therefore it calls for focused attention in formulating the JV agreement clauses;

u The scope of business and its term, exclusivity as between the partners with no competing businesses in territories and time-frame fixed, its location and sourcing of the required facilities, technology inputs with secure IPRs and rewards in putting them to work in India, estimated investment and sourcing; capital structure of the company and timing the funding in the agreed ratio between the joint venture partners and others like the financial institutions; the volume of products and services to be generated and the capacity scaling, quality assurance, marketing strategies for product promotion and launches and channels in domestic and foreign trade, the size and the proportion, usually equal, of the representation of the JV partners on the Board with the executive powers and functions divided between the Chairman/Vice-Chairman combined with or independent of the managing director/joint managing director structured to achieve balanced and smooth operations in mutual trust and confidence of the JV partners, frequency of Board meetings and the manner of decision-making at Board level with core subjects reserved for prior clearance by both the groups forming the JV partners but armed with veto powers after due consultations; exercise of the plenary powers of management as the last word in a workable division of subjects in balanced power-sharing; sub-quorum of directors’ presence at the Board meetings from the sides; selection and sourcing of material inputs with purchasing powers clearly spelt out; participation of expatriate nominees of the foreign JV partner taking residence in India occupying key positions parallel with the Indian partner’s nominees (who between them make or mar the success of the JV and working regularly but with loyalty and reporting to the JV partner who nominated them instantly on the goings on in India and abroad) selection and procurement procedures of the inputs in a supply-chain and inventory management with Enterprise Resource Planning capsules; profit-sharing and dividend policy; future projects or expansion through acquisitions and the rights of first refusal; Treasury management, accounting and accounts control with control on costs and prices;

u Regulatory and compliance management and assumption of responsibilities and answerability in their respective countries with mutual co-operation;

10.7 Lifting of the Veil - Instances of lifting corporate veil under the provisions of the Act

Examples in the Act itself

10.7-1 At least fifteen instances where the corporate veil is lifted disregarding the corporate personality of a company are found in the Act itself:

u Section 4 giving the meaning of ‘holding company’ and ‘subsidiary’ allowing one company to have control on the composition of the board of directors of another company thereby making its own subsidiary company;

u Section 212 requiring that the annual report of the subsidiary companies shall be attached to the balance sheet of the holding company in addition to giving certain other particulars pertaining to the interest of the holding company in the subsidiaries in its annual report each year (as group accounts);

u Section 214(1) conferring rights on the holding company’s representatives authorized by its resolution (whether of board or of the general body) to inspect the books of account kept by any of its subsidiaries at any time during business hours;

u Section 214(2) conferring the right to move the Company Law Board allowed to the members of a company under section 235 on the members of the holding company as if they alone were members of the subsidiary seeking its order that the affairs of the subsidiary ought to be investigated by an inspector or inspectors at which the Central Government shall be bound to appoint one or more competent persons as inspectors to investigate the affairs of the subsidiary and to report thereon as directed by the Central Government;

u Section 45 rendering the residuary members in a company severally liable for the whole debts made by a company in a period of more than six months during which the company carried on business with the number of members reduced to less than the number prescribed as the legal minimum in the knowledge of the residuary members;

