petitioner that he had not received notices for any of the meetings and that no meetings were convened by the respondents were ill-conceived and must fail. The grievances of the petitioners in regard to the substitution of the fourth respondent to be the foreign collaborator in the place of the sixth respondent had to be considered in the light of the JVA entered into between the first petitioner and the sixth respondent. Clause 3.3 of the JVA stipulated that the sixth respondent should hold 90 per cent of the equity shares of the company and the first petitioner should hold the remaining 10 per cent of the equity shares. It was, therefore, clear that the shares of the company allotted to either of the parties to the JVA might at the discretion of such party be allotted to any of its affiliates, of course, subject to the approval of the competent authority. It was obvious from the JVA that for any such allotment by any party to the JVA in favour of the affiliates, concurrence of the other party was not contemplated. It was on record that the Secretariat for Industrial Approvals (SIA) by its communication dated 12-9-1996 conveyed its approval in favour of the company for foreign collaboration subject to certain terms and conditions. Accordingly, the sixth respondent should be the foreign collaborator of the company, who held 90 per cent in the paid-up capital of Rs. 3.75 lakhs of the company. The company by its letter dated 7-5-1999 made an application before the SIA for substitution of the fourth respondent in the place of the sixth respondent to invest in the company. The company had categorically stated that as per the JVA, the shares were to be allotted to the sixth respondent or its affiliates. The fourth respondent, an affiliate of the sixth respondent, the fact of which was denied by the petitioners, made investments aggregating to Rs. 443.26 lakhs in the project enabling the company to operate the school at Bangalore. In the circumstances, the company had sought permission to allot 33,75,000 shares of Rs. 10 each in favour of the fourth respondent and returned Rs.68.26 lakhs, the excess sum over and above the original approved amount of foreign equity of Rs. 375 lakhs that was already brought in by the fourth respondent. The company had further sought to engage the sixth respondent to provide technical services to run the school on payment of fee of US $ 75,000 per annum for a period of two years. The SIA by its communication dated 14-6-1999 had conveyed its approval for the amendments proposed by the company on the premises that the fourth respondent was an affiliate of the sixth respondent. Admittedly, the sixth respondent was held by respondent Nos. 2 and 5. The telefax dated 21-5-2002 sent by the tenth respondent showed that the fourth respondent was wholly owned by the second respondent, in which case the plea of the petitioners that the fourth respondent was not an affiliate of the sixth respondent must fail

