 inferred that in order for a FVCI (which falls within the purview of the definition of VCC of the Income-tax Act) to claim exemption on income from its investment in a VCU, it would be necessary that the investment in the VCU falls under the above nine sectors. If that is not the case, the income of the FVCI would be taxable in India subject to provisions of the DTAA between India and the country of which the FVCI is a resident. The Income-tax Act provides that the investors who receive any income (by way of dividends, interest, capital gain by alienation of the units/shares) out of investments made in a VCC is liable to income-tax. This means that those who invest in the FVCI would be liable to income-tax in the manner as if the investor had directly invested in the Indian company or VCU. The taxability of the FVCI and its investors would significantly change if the FVCI invests into a VCC/VCF which, in turn, invests in VCU engaged in exempted sectors.

DTAA

3. The Income-tax Act provides that where there exists a Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) between India and another foreign country, residents of that foreign country may choose to be assessed in India under the Income-tax Act to the extent it is more beneficial to the assessee. If the provisions of the DTAA are more favourable than the Income-tax Act, the DTAA would apply.

Therefore, it is important to decide upon the structure of FVCI and strategize its entry into India keeping into context the provisions of the DTAA between Indian and the country where the FVCI would be a resident.

4. Direct Tax Code

4.1 TAXATION OF VENTURE CAPITAL COMPANY AND VENTURE CAPITAL FUND UNDER DTC - Chapter VI of the Direct Tax Code (DTC) deals with provisions of tax on income received from venture capital company and venture capital fund and clause 108(1) of the DTC provides as follows:

'Notwithstanding anything in this Code, any income received by a person out of investments made in a venture capital company or venture capital fund shall be chargeable to income-tax in the same manner as if it were the income received by such person had he made investments directly in the venture capital undertaking.'

4.2 AMENDED DEFINITION OF VENTURE CAPITAL UNDERTAKING IN DTC - The DTC has retained the definition of 'venture capital company' and 'venture capital fund' in terms of the present definition of the said terms as per the Income-tax Act. However, it has defined a 'venture capital undertaking' 19 to mean:

'such domestic company whose shares are not listed in a recognised stock exchange in India and which is engaged in the business of—

(a) nanotechnology;

(b) information technology relating to hardware and software development;

(c) seed research and development;

(d) bio-technology;

(e) research and development of new chemical entities in the pharmaceutical sector;

(f) production of bio-fuels;

(g) dairy or poultry;

(h) building and operating composite hotel-cum-convention centre with seating capacity of more than three thousand;

(i) development of infrastructure facility; or

(j) any other business as may be prescribed'

The Direct Tax Code (DTC), similar to the Income-tax Act, provides that any income of a venture capital company or a venture capital fund from investment in a venture capital undertaking shall be exempted from payment of income-tax 20. Thus, reading clause 108(1) along with this clause would imply that the consequences of taxation of a FVCI on its income derived form investing in VCUs is similar to those under the Income-tax Act in its current form as aforesaid. However, the DTC by amending the definition of VCU has contemplated to enlarge the scope of exempted sectors.

Conclusion

5. In the light of the above discussions it is important for an overseas investor to examine the structuring of the investment. It is advisable that local advice is sought in this regard. There are several FVCI's operating in the Indian market. It is generally observed that many of them operate through a vehicle (usually a company) incorporated in Mauritius, Cyprus or Singapore investing in India. This route is usually preferred, inter alia, due to issues related to tax (capital gains, business profits, dividends) arising out of the DTAA between India and the said countries. For example, transfer of shares of an Indian company held by a company incorporated in Mauritius is exempt from paying tax on capital gains (if any) in India and may be liable to pay tax in Mauritius as per the DTAA between India and Mauritius 21 whereas a company incorporated in the USA would have to pay tax on capital gains in India and may also be taxed in the USA as per the DTAA between India and the USA. However, recent Court decisions have upheld actions taken by regulators against certain companies by piercing the corporate veil to investigate the role, nature, objective of companies operating out of so-called tax haven destinations 22 . The Government of India is also actively entering into Tax Information Exchange Agreements with various countries like Bahamas, Bermuda, the British Virgin Islands and the Isle of Man in order to avoid tax evasions and money laundering.

