As already mentioned, the sector of JVs is essentially of the latest overseas-technology oriented brought into instant use in India without its transfer for licenced production to an Indian party, a concept of immense value in technological knowledge-sharing and in saving the time otherwise taken in indigenisation and the related sunk costs both lending to give a competitive edge. In order to afford greater role in the indigenous manufacture, a policy change was announced by the Government in 2002 allowing 26% FDI with a view to throwing open a part of the Rs. 40,000 crore annual defence procurement sourcing by imports to Indian manufacturing companies, and some ten of them including Mahindra & Mahindra, L&T and Tata Motors that claimed capability to make body armour, bullet-proof clothing, mobile launchers, avionics, simulators, radars and special purpose vehicles etc. were issued LOIs. It would, however, appear that there has been no progress in the last three years and no JVs have been formed as the methodology of implementation of the change in the policy is under examination by Mr. Vijay Kelkar who has been entrusted with it.

